---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
---
<Page
  title="Getting started — Zonesteward docs"
  description="Connect a Cloudflare token with exactly the permissions you're comfortable with, pick your zones, ask a question, approve a change."
  eyebrow="Docs"
  h1="Getting started."
  lede="Four steps, ten minutes. Start read-only if you want to; you can grant more later without reconnecting."
>
  <h2>0. Take the tour</h2>
  <p>
    On first sign-in the product runs a two-minute guided tour that drives
    itself: it asks a question, reads the answer off the globe, proposes a
    change and stops at the approval card. Let it run once. Everything below
    will already look familiar.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Connect a Cloudflare token</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare has no OAuth for third-party apps, so you create an API token
    yourself. Zonesteward's connection wizard builds a <strong>pre-filled
    token-creation link</strong> with exactly the permission groups it needs, in
    three tiers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core</strong> (required) — zones and analytics, read and write for WAF, cache and rate-limit rules, cache purge, DNS and zone settings, and the token's view of its own permissions. Enough for every question, the globe, and approved changes to rules, DNS and settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Operate</strong> — adds redirects, transform and header rules, config and origin rules, cache settings, IP access rules and lockdowns, SSL and certificates, managed rulesets and bulk redirects.</li>
    <li><strong>Extended</strong> — adds health checks and load balancers, Logpush, Cloudflare notifications, waiting rooms, Turnstile, email routing, Workers and Access visibility, account-wide analytics, and creating or pausing zones.</li>
  </ul>
  <Shot shot="connect-tiers" alt="Connecting Cloudflare: the three permission tiers a new token can have — Core (required), Operate and Extended — with what each one allows" caption="The three tiers, as the wizard shows them" sizes="(min-width: 760px) 640px, 100vw" />
  <p>
    Prefer to start read-only? Create a token with only read permissions
    yourself and paste it. Every write action stays unavailable until the
    token can do it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paste the token back and the wizard introspects it and tells you precisely
    what it can and can't do. Permissions are <em>never</em> discovered by
    attempting a write. If you grant more later, re-check in Settings → Tokens
    and it takes effect without reconnecting.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">Use a dedicated token per Cloudflare account, scoped to the zones you want visible. Tokens are encrypted at rest and never leave the server. <a href="/security">How they're held.</a></p>

  <h2>2. Pick your zones</h2>
  <p>
    Every zone the token can see appears in the fleet roster with its plan tier.
    Group them — by client, by environment, however you think about them.
    Groups drive bulk changes and share-link scoping later.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare gates parts of its analytics API by plan. Free zones can't return
    per-colo data, some datasets are Pro and above, and retention is shorter.
    Zonesteward treats all of that as expected and degrades per zone rather than
    failing the batch; the fleet audit in Settings → Developer shows you exactly
    what each zone can and can't answer.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Ask something</h2>
  <p>Start with the question you'd ask a colleague:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>Who is attacking us right now?</em></li>
    <li><em>Why is example.com slow from Germany this week?</em></li>
    <li><em>Which zones are still on TLS 1.0?</em></li>
    <li><em>Show me every 5xx in the last hour, by path.</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/use-cases">Ten of these, with what it checks and what it proposes →</a></p>
  <Shot shot="tour-ask" alt="Zonesteward chat: a 24-hour health answer on the left, a firewall activity chart on the right with Viz, Table, GraphQL and JSON tabs" caption="An answer and its chart, with the query one tab away" sizes="(min-width: 760px) 640px, 100vw" />
  <p>
    The model writes the GraphQL; the server scopes it to your active zone or
    group. Answers drop into the conversation with their evidence, so the next
    question starts from data.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Approve a change</h2>
  <p>
    Ask for a change — <em>"challenge that IP on every client zone"</em> — and
    you get an approval card: the zone, the exact change, its impact level, and
    for firewall rules how many of your last 24 hours' requests it would have
    matched. Approve or reject. The server checks nothing moved since you
    looked, executes, reads the result back from Cloudflare, and records how to
    undo it. <a href="/how-it-works">The whole pipeline.</a>
  </p>
  <Shot shot="gate-card" alt="Approval card: Create WAF rule (managed challenge) on WordPress login and XML-RPC endpoints — would have matched about 115 requests of the last 24 hours, broken down by path, network, country and user agent, with a map, and Approve & run or Reject" caption="An approval card" sizes="(min-width: 760px) 640px, 100vw" />

  <h2>5. Turn a question into an alert</h2>
  <p>
    Anything you can ask about, you can watch. This one lives on its own page,
    not in the chat: open Alerts → Rules, describe what would worry you — <em>"tell me if cache hit ratio on any client zone
    drops below 60% for half an hour"</em> — and the model composes the rule:
    the query, the threshold, the window, a minimum volume so quiet hours can't
    trip it. It's dry-run against the zone before you arm it, and the preview
    tells you in plain words what it will do. Pick the channels, arm it, done.
  </p>
  <Shot shot="tour-alerts" alt="Alert rules page: a prompt box to build a rule with AI, and two rules — site down on 5xx rate, origin unreachable — each explained in plain words with its threshold, window and channels" caption="Alerts → Rules" sizes="(min-width: 760px) 640px, 100vw" />

  <h2>Bring your own model</h2>
  <p>
    Zonesteward calls the model provider you choose, under your own key, so
    usage is billed to you at the provider's rates and never marked up.
    Settings → AI takes Anthropic, OpenAI, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint —
    including one you host, if you'd rather nothing leave your network. Pick a
    model per job, then <em>Test and save</em>: it makes one tiny call that
    asks the model for structured output, because a model that can't do that
    can't compose graphs or alert rules, and you'd rather learn that now than
    mid-incident. Keys are stored the same way Cloudflare tokens are.
  </p>

  <h2>Need more?</h2>
  <p>
    Fuller docs are coming as the beta shapes them. Until then,
    <a href="mailto:hello@zonesteward.com">email</a> — in beta you're talking to
    the person who wrote the code.
  </p>
</Page>
